==================================================
SPRINT 5B OBJECTIVE
==================================================

Implement the actual:

AG-002 Memory Consolidation Engine

The engine must safely identify related/duplicate memory records
and create consolidated memory records according to explicit
policies.

Consolidation must be:

- deterministic
- authorization-aware
- lifecycle-aware
- security-aware
- version-aware
- namespace-aware
- non-destructive
- auditable
- testable
- dependency-injected

This is NOT an LLM summarization system.

Do NOT generate semantic summaries using an AI model.

Use deterministic consolidation only.

==================================================
1. ARCHITECTURE INSPECTION
==================================================

Before implementation, identify and reuse existing:

- MemoryRecord
- MemoryType
- MemoryPriority
- MemoryState/Lifecycle state
- MemoryRepository
- repository update/create methods
- AuthorizationService
- MemoryActor
- security policies
- lifecycle service
- versioning mechanism
- event system
- sanitization utilities
- configuration system
- typed AG-002 errors
- clock abstraction

Do NOT create duplicate versions of these systems.

If a required abstraction already exists, reuse it.

==================================================
2. CONSOLIDATION SERVICE CONTRACT
==================================================

Create an interface such as:

MemoryConsolidationService

and an implementation such as:

MemoryConsolidationServiceImpl

Use the naming conventions already established in AG-002.

The service should expose deterministic operations for:

- finding consolidation candidates
- evaluating whether candidates may be consolidated
- consolidating compatible records
- reporting consolidation results/statistics

Use dependency injection.

Do not hard-code repositories or global services.

==================================================
3. CONSOLIDATION REQUEST
==================================================

The request should contain the necessary actor/security context
and consolidation scope.

Where appropriate support:

- actor
- namespace
- memory types
- project
- workspace
- organization
- candidate records or candidate query
- consolidation policy
- correlation/request ID

Reuse existing actor/scope contracts.

Do not invent duplicate actor models.

==================================================
4. CANDIDATE DETECTION
==================================================

Implement deterministic candidate detection.

Candidates may be related through existing memory identity
semantics such as:

- namespace
- key
- memory type
- project/workspace scope
- compatible metadata

Do NOT perform fuzzy semantic similarity using an LLM.

Do NOT use embeddings.

Candidate detection must be explainable and deterministic.

Every candidate decision should have a clear reason.

==================================================
5. CONSOLIDATION POLICY
==================================================

Implement an explicit validated policy.

Policy should support at least:

- enabled/disabled
- minimum candidate count
- maximum records per operation
- allowed memory types
- priority behavior
- version behavior
- retention behavior
- security behavior
- namespace restrictions
- source preservation
- output priority rules

Use Zod validation following existing AG-002 configuration
patterns.

Use sensible defaults.

Do not create unnecessary configuration.

==================================================
6. NON-DESTRUCTIVE GUARANTEE
==================================================

THIS IS CRITICAL.

Consolidation MUST NOT automatically delete source records.

After successful consolidation:

- source records remain available
- source versions remain intact
- source metadata is preserved
- source audit history remains intact

If the architecture supports an explicit archive transition,
it may be used ONLY if the policy explicitly allows it and
authorization is satisfied.

Default behavior must be non-destructive.

==================================================
7. CONSOLIDATED RECORD
==================================================

Create a deterministic consolidated record.

The output must preserve appropriate:

- memory type
- namespace
- scope
- priority
- security level
- retention characteristics
- timestamps
- source references
- provenance
- version information

The output must NOT contain secrets that were prohibited from
the source/context boundary.

Do not overwrite an unrelated existing record.

Use a deterministic identity/key strategy consistent with the
existing memory architecture.

Do NOT introduce a completely separate identity system.

==================================================
8. PRIORITY RULES
==================================================

When combining records:

CRITICAL > HIGH > MEDIUM > LOW

The consolidated record must never downgrade a higher-priority
source because of a lower-priority source.

Test combinations such as:

CRITICAL + LOW
HIGH + MEDIUM
MEDIUM + LOW
CRITICAL + HIGH

==================================================
9. SECURITY
==================================================

Every candidate must pass authorization.

Consolidation must never combine memories across unauthorized:

- organizations
- workspaces
- projects
- namespaces
- security levels

Missing/invalid actor context must fail closed.

A user with insufficient security clearance must not be able
to consolidate protected records.

Do not rely only on the caller to filter candidates.

The consolidation service itself must enforce security.

==================================================
10. LIFECYCLE
==================================================

Do not consolidate:

- DELETED
- EXPIRED

records.

Respect ARCHIVED according to the existing architecture and
policy.

Do not revive expired/deleted memories.

Lifecycle checks must happen before consolidation.

==================================================
11. VERSIONING / CONCURRENCY
==================================================

Use the existing version-safe repository mechanisms.

The consolidation operation must safely handle:

- stale source versions
- concurrent updates
- version conflicts
- duplicate consolidation attempts

A version conflict must not silently overwrite newer data.

Use existing typed errors.

Mark retryable vs non-retryable appropriately.

==================================================
12. IDEMPOTENCY
==================================================

Where supported by the existing architecture, consolidation
should be idempotent.

Running the same consolidation request twice should not create
unbounded duplicate consolidated records.

Use deterministic source references / identity.

Do NOT invent an unrelated idempotency subsystem.

==================================================
13. SANITIZATION
==================================================

Sensitive information must not leak into:

- consolidated records
- source references if they contain content
- event payloads
- statistics
- logs
- errors

Reuse the canonical AG-002 sanitizer.

Test at minimum:

- apiKey
- password
- token
- secret
- credential
- pwd
- passphrase

Test nested and mixed-case variants.

==================================================
14. PROVENANCE
==================================================

The consolidated record should preserve provenance sufficient
to understand which source memories contributed to it.

Use safe references/identifiers.

Do NOT copy sensitive source contents into provenance metadata.

Provenance should be deterministic.

==================================================
15. EVENTS
==================================================

Use the existing AG-002 event infrastructure.

Emit appropriate consolidation events if the architecture
requires them.

Events should contain safe:

- event type
- consolidation ID
- actor ID/type where allowed
- source IDs
- output ID
- timestamp
- correlation ID
- status/result

Never include raw secrets or sensitive memory content.

If an existing event type is appropriate, reuse it rather than
creating a duplicate event.

==================================================
16. STATISTICS
==================================================

Return useful deterministic statistics such as:

- candidates discovered
- candidates authorized
- candidates rejected
- candidates filtered by lifecycle
- candidates filtered by scope
- candidates filtered by security
- groups formed
- groups consolidated
- records created
- records preserved
- conflicts
- skipped groups
- duration

Statistics must not expose memory contents or secrets.

==================================================
17. ERROR HANDLING
==================================================

